        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The NASA 7 mission began with the launch of the Space Shuttle Endeavour (STS-89) on January 22, 1998.  STS-89, the eighth docking mission between the U.S. Space Shuttle and Russia's Mir Space Station, carried a double Spacehab facility and seven crewmembers to Mir, including mission specialist Dr. Andrew Thomas.  Dr. Thomas joined the Mir 24 crew, replacing David Wolf as the seventh U.S. long-duration astronaut to stay aboard the Russian space station Mir.  The Mir 25 crew, commander Talgat Musabayev and flight engineer Nikolai Budarin, launched in February 1998 to join Dr. Thomas on Mir and replace the Mir 24 crew.  <P>During the docked phase of STS-89, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred from the Mir space station to the Shuttle science samples collected by the Mir 24/NASA 6 crew.  After return to Earth, the samples were analyzed by ground-based researchers.  The Shuttle crew also transferred hardware and supplies to Mir in support of the health and well being of the Mir 25/NASA 7 crew, station maintenance, and future investigations on the Mir.  This research focused on studies in the areas of human life sciences, microgravity sciences, space biology, advanced technology, Earth sciences, and International Space Station (ISS) risk mitigation.<P>Human Life Sciences research during the Mir 24/Mir 25/NASA 7 mission included experiments investigating human responses to long duration space flight and readaptation to Earth gravity in the areas of bone mineral density, muscle volume, crew interactions, immune response, autonomic function, and the role of sleep in circadian rhythms, vestibular function and immune status.  In addition, environmental factors were assessed, including air and water quality, surface microbiology and crew microbiology.<P>The commercially initiated research of the Advanced Technology discipline was aimed at evaluating new technologies and techniques using the Mir space station and the Shuttle as unique testing environments. Experiments were designed to test plant growth in microgravity within a special chamber and to measure the sensitivity of detector components to space radiation within a vehicle.   The knowledge gained has the potential to significantly improve aspects of commercial processing here on Earth.<P>One group of experiments specifically prepared NASA for building and operating the upcoming International Space Station.  These investigations were used as a base for the design and operation of the International Space Station (ISS) and consisted of several technology demonstrations associated with human factors and maintenance of crew health and safety aboard the station.  To improve the design and operation of the ISS, information on the Mir interior and exterior environments was gathered, and the structure and design evaluated.  Other studies included measuring radiation and testing computer hardware.<P>Microgravity research had the general goal of advancing scientific understanding through research in biotechnology, crystal growth and materials science.  Microgravity research included the study of protein crystal growth, basic cell-to-cell interactions and characterization of the effect of gravity on alloys and granular materials.  The ambient acceleration and vibration environment of Mir was also characterized to support future research programs.<P>To better understand our Earth and solar system, research in ocean biochemistry, land surface hydrology, and meteorology was performed.  Observation and documentation of transient natural and human-induced changes was accomplished with the use of hand-held photography.  Earth observation and photography from Mir allows for documentation of atmospheric conditions, ecological changes and seasonal changes over long time periods, as well as during ephemeral events.  In addition, window surveys were performed to document the condition and degradation of the Mir window surfaces used for Earth observations.<P>Nearly all Shuttle-Mir investigations were joint efforts between the U.S. and Russia.  In addition to the benefits of international cooperation, the NASA 7 mission added to the understanding of many scientific areas, including how living organisms are affected by long-duration space flight.  <P>Andy Thomas returned home on the Space Shuttle Discovery (STS-91), which launched on June 2, 1998.  This final docking mission successfully concluded Phase 1 of the International Space Station program.  The Phase 1 program was a difficult and unique program because of its international scope and length of mission duration, but it provided both an excellent scientific return and a training ground for future operations on the International Space Station.<END>
